-- title:
--   Weight-zero mod-p forms are reductions of classical forms
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime with $5 \le p$, let $N \ge 1$ be an integer with $p \nmid N$, and let $K$ be a field of characteristic $p$. Let $G$ be an element of the intermediate field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ K\ N$ of $K((q)) =$ `LaurentSeries K`, that is, of the subfield generated over $K$ by the two series $\mathrm{jqModC}\ K = q^{-1}\cdot(\text{the }K\text{-reduction of the integral power series }\mathrm{jNum})$ and its $q \mapsto q^N$ substitution $\mathrm{jqNModC}\ K\ N$. Assume `IsModPFormFn K 0 G`, which at $m = 0$ says that $G^{6}$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra generated by $\mathrm{jqModC}\ K$ and that $G^{2}$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra generated by $(\mathrm{jqModC}\ K)^{-1}$. Then there is a power series $\varphi \in K[[q]]$ lying in the $K$-submodule $\mathrm{modPMod}\ N\ (2\cdot 0)\ K$ — the $K$-span of those power series $\sum_n a_n q^n$ with $a_n \in \mathbb{Z}$ whose images in $K$ are the reductions of the integers $a_n$ arising as the $q$-expansion coefficients $\mathrm{qCoeff}\ f\ n$ of some weight-$2\cdot 0$ modular form $f$ on $\Gamma_0(N)$ — such that the image of $\varphi$ in $K((q))$ equals $\mathrm{qexpOfWeight}\ K\ 0\ G = G \cdot (\mathrm{thetaJ}\ K)^{0}$, i.e. equals $G$ itself.
--
--   This is the weight-$0$ instance of the passage from Katz-style integrality conditions on a $q$-series to membership in the mod-$p$ reduction of the space of classical modular forms of level $\Gamma_0(N)$; it is invoked to settle the $m = 0$ branch of [`ModPForms.exists_mem_modPMod_ofPowerSeries_eq_qexpOfWeight_of_isModPFormFn`](thm.html#ModPForms.exists_mem_modPMod_ofPowerSeries_eq_qexpOfWeight_of_isModPFormFn).
